.TH INIT 8
.SH NAME
init \- the first program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /bin/rc /lib/init
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Init
is the last thing the kernel starts, once every self-test has run.
It is not a program of its own.
It is
.BR apps/rc/init ,
an ordinary
.IR rc (1)
script staged onto the disk by the build and run as
.BR /bin/rc " " /lib/init ,
with the console as its three descriptors.
The kernel does nothing more once it starts: what
.I init
does is the script's business.
.PP
The script starts the keyboard server, waits for its name to appear,
and mounts it:
.IP
.EX
kbdfs &
while(! test -e /srv/kbdfs) sleep 1
mount /srv/kbdfs /n/kbd
.EE
.PP
.IR Kbdfs (4)
opens
.BR /dev/scancode ,
which diverts the keyboard out of the console's own line discipline
into a file it translates and serves.
This is why the step exists.
The draw server has to read the keyboard raw, and if it read
.B /dev/cons
for that, the serial line sharing that console would go raw with it,
leaving the serial shell below with no line discipline of its own.
Diverting the keyboard through a file the draw server reads instead
keeps the console cooked, echoing, and free for the serial shell alone.
.PP
The draw server starts next, reading its keys from the file just
mounted, and the script waits for it to post its name before going on:
.IP
.EX
intuition /n/kbd/kbd &
while(! test -e /srv/draw) sleep 1
.EE
.PP
Two client programs follow, both left running in the background:
.IP
.EX
terminal &
muidemo &
.EE
.PP
.I Terminal ,
described in
.IR window (1),
is a window with a shell in it, the one on the screen a user without a
serial cable gets.
.I Muidemo
is a panel of gadgets built on
.B sys/libmui
that gives the desktop something drawn with the toolkit besides a
shell.
.PP
The last two lines are
.I init
itself:
.IP
.EX
cd $home
exec rc -i
.EE
.PP
.I Exec
replaces the script in place, so
.I init
becomes an interactive
.I rc
on the console rather than starting one as a child.
This is the serial shell.
.SS rcmain
Before its first command, every
.I rc
reads
.BR /lib/rcmain ,
staged from
.BR apps/rc/rcmain .
It sets four variables, each only if the environment did not already
hold one:
.IP
.EX
path=(. /bin)
home=/usr/glenda
ifs=(' ' '\et' '\en')
prompt=('% ' '\et')
.EE
.PP
Every default yields to what
.B /env
already held when the shell started, so a variable set before
.I rc
runs is never overwritten.
.SS Two shells
The machine ends up with two interactive shells, on two consoles.
The serial line is
.IR init 's
own
.IR rc ,
reading and writing
.BR /dev/cons
with the kernel's line discipline: backspace erases a character,
control-U erases the line, control-D ends the line or, on an empty
line, ends the file.
The window is
.IR terminal 's
child
.IR rc ,
reading and writing two pipes that
.I terminal
turns into a grid of cells on the screen.
A window cooks its own lines, in
.BR sys/libedit ,
and adds control-W and the arrow keys that the console's own
discipline does not have.
.PP
Each shell is oblivious to the other.
A command typed at one does not appear on the other, and a fault or an
.B exit
in one leaves the other running.
.SS The process picture
Bringing up two shells this way costs more processes than Plan 9 spends
on the same picture, because a Vectra process cannot wait on two
descriptors at once and forks a reader instead wherever a server has
two blocking sources.
.IR Fatfs (4)
and
.IR kfs (4)
are one process each.
The serial shell is one.
.IR Kbdfs (4)
and
.IR intuition (4)
are each a proc of threads: a server and an I/O proc for the pipe or
scancode stream it reads.
.I Terminal
is the same shape, plus the
.I rc
it starts.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/init
the script above, staged from
.B apps/rc/init
.TP
.B /lib/rcmain
staged from
.B apps/rc/rcmain
.TP
.B /dev/scancode
the raw keyboard,
.IR kbdfs (4)'s
source
.SH SOURCE
.B apps/rc/init
.br
.B apps/rc/rcmain
.br
.B kernel/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rc (1),
.IR window (1),
.IR muidemo (1),
.IR boot (8),
.IR kbdfs (4),
.IR intuition (4),
.IR fatfs (4),
.IR kfs (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A machine with no
.B /lib/init
logs a warning and idles with no shell on any console.
See
.IR boot (8).
A machine with no keyboard controller, either
.B virt
board, still gets the window, drawn and waiting, but nothing can type
into it.
The serial line is that machine's only keyboard.
